[{:seon.task/name "every-fork-server-wait-ends-with-the-server"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 by the stability sweep (notes/stability-waits-2026-10-25.md), checked against the code. A wait on the fork server ends only on its answer. (1) `seon.fork-server/promote` with no live standby answers :fork-server-lost and keeps :waiters: each queued :ext request (:stats, :static) is never answered, and a request arriving after the loss is queued with nothing to send it to (:ext sends only when :pid). The loss must answer every waiter, and every later request, with the declared error (docs/failure-points.md, the fork server). A fork asked after the loss is the same wait: the :cmd fork was neither sent nor answered, and the attach that asked for it hung (an agent test's root whose fork server died at boot, add-deps refusing a jar). Both answer at once: a waiter with `seon.fork-server/lost` (:seon.root/refused), a fork with :fork-failed naming it, its socket end closed. The other two waits are `seon.fork-server/an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-lost-fork-server-answers-every-waiter}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 by the stability sweep (notes/stability-waits-2026-10-25.md), checked against the code; split from `seon.fork-server/every-fork-server-wait-ends-with-the-server`, which answers a lost server's waits. (1) :stats and :static answers pair with :waiters by FIFO (`peek`). After a promote the standby is sent every unanswered request again, so FIFO holds only while every answer the dead server wrote arrived whole before its EOF; an answer cut by the death pairs the next waiter with the wrong request. The request carries its :rid to the server and the answer echoes it, and the proc answers the waiter by :rid. (2) `seon.fork-server/release!` waits for the boot server's exit with a blocking `c-waitpid` (no WNOHANG), so a wedged server (a deadlocked child, docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads) hangs root's stop; then `.join 5000` on the event reader is a clock deciding (§4). Stop is a kill of the server's process group (as a root's processes end with it, AGENTS.md §8) followed by the reap and the reader's EOF, and no timeout. Deletes the join's timeout; replaces FIFO pairing with :rid pairing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-promoted-servers-answers-pair-by-request-id seon.procs-test/stop-ends-a-wedged-fork-server}}]
